WEBVTT

s1.1
00:00:00.650 --> 00:00:03.173
Symphony treats agent code as untrusted.

s1.2
00:00:03.173 --> 00:00:07.225
People run the CLI and the Tern plugin, with an API token.

s1.3
00:00:07.225 --> 00:00:10.363
The edge is a Cloudflare Tunnel. It only dials out.

s1.4
00:00:10.363 --> 00:00:15.136
The trusted side runs the API, the daemon, the runners, the merger and Redis.

s1.5
00:00:15.136 --> 00:00:19.120
It keeps the integration credentials, and makes every write that needs them.

s1.6
00:00:19.120 --> 00:00:24.485
The agent runs in a workspace pod, which gets only the credentials of its own attempt.

s2.1
00:00:24.835 --> 00:00:27.761
Agents call model providers with API keys.

s2.2
00:00:27.761 --> 00:00:34.044
Host cloud credentials, such as Amazon Bedrock, and custom endpoints with no authentication also work.

s2.3
00:00:34.044 --> 00:00:37.694
Symphony has no provider OAuth and no subscription login.

s2.4
00:00:37.694 --> 00:00:40.665
An admin stores a key from Tern or the CLI.

s2.5
00:00:40.665 --> 00:00:44.320
Keys are write-only. Tern shows only whether a key is set.

s3.1
00:00:45.170 --> 00:00:47.571
Each attempt runs in its own workspace pod.

s3.2
00:00:47.571 --> 00:00:53.708
Its NetworkPolicy allows no ingress, and egress only to the gateway, DNS and public addresses.

s3.3
00:00:53.708 --> 00:00:59.014
Private ranges are excluded: no API, Redis, node or metadata endpoint.

s3.4
00:00:59.014 --> 00:01:03.843
The pod mounts no ServiceAccount token, so it cannot call the Kubernetes API.

s3.5
00:01:03.843 --> 00:01:07.199
Nothing connects into the pod. The runner uses pods/exec.

s3.6
00:01:07.199 --> 00:01:12.006
Public egress is open, so agent code can leak only its own credentials.

s4.1
00:01:12.356 --> 00:01:14.288
A workspace gets four credentials.

s4.2
00:01:14.288 --> 00:01:19.008
The model credential of its role, and a GitHub token for the ticket's repositories.

s4.3
00:01:19.008 --> 00:01:24.437
The telemetry token of the dispatch, and one MCP token for each agent session.

s4.4
00:01:24.437 --> 00:01:26.622
The runner writes them into private files.

s4.5
00:01:26.622 --> 00:01:32.573
The pod never holds the Linear key, the App private key, chat tokens or API tokens.

s5.1
00:01:33.423 --> 00:01:37.422
A normal ticket gets the GitHub scope ghapp.WORKSPACE.

s5.2
00:01:37.422 --> 00:01:41.627
It writes code and pull requests, and reads checks, actions and statuses.

s5.3
00:01:41.627 --> 00:01:44.552
It has no issues, projects or workflows permission.

s5.4
00:01:44.552 --> 00:01:50.624
An investigation gets ghapp.READ_ONLY, so GitHub refuses its push or pull request.

s5.5
00:01:50.624 --> 00:01:54.914
A token lives at most one hour. The runner renews it between turns.

s6.1
00:01:55.264 --> 00:01:58.583
An admin picks MCP servers for each agent role.

s6.2
00:01:58.583 --> 00:02:02.234
Their secret headers live in a Secret that only the gateway mounts.

s6.3
00:02:02.234 --> 00:02:06.234
The gateway drops the agent's Authorization and adds the stored headers.

s6.4
00:02:06.234 --> 00:02:10.101
Each agent session gets its own MCP token, for one role.

s6.5
00:02:10.101 --> 00:02:13.286
Another role, or a disabled server, gets a 403.

s6.6
00:02:13.286 --> 00:02:15.666
The token is revoked when the session ends.

s6.7
00:02:15.666 --> 00:02:21.370
Symphony ignores repository MCP configs, and turns tools.xdev off.

s7.1
00:02:22.220 --> 00:02:26.853
Setup checks each integration credential with its service, then saves it in a Secret.

s7.2
00:02:26.853 --> 00:02:30.621
Only an admin can write them, and no response or log shows them.

s7.3
00:02:30.621 --> 00:02:36.483
Each identity has a namespaced Role. The API only patches Secrets that exist before setup.

s7.4
00:02:36.483 --> 00:02:40.521
Webhooks need a valid HMAC signature, and they only wake a loop.

s7.5
00:02:40.521 --> 00:02:44.914
Fencing makes sure that only the current dispatch of a ticket can change state.

s8.1
00:02:45.264 --> 00:02:48.327
API tokens have three roles. A viewer reads.

s8.2
00:02:48.327 --> 00:02:52.180
An operator also acts: pause, hold, interrupt and bypass.

s8.3
00:02:52.180 --> 00:02:55.781
An admin also manages tokens, provider keys and setup.

s8.4
00:02:55.781 --> 00:02:59.693
The API stores only a SHA-256 hash of each secret.

s8.5
00:02:59.693 --> 00:03:05.256
A token gets 600 requests a minute. Above a limit, the API answers 429.

s8.6
00:03:05.256 --> 00:03:09.293
The API audits every command and every provider change.

s9.1
00:03:10.543 --> 00:03:14.835
The assistant answers in Slack and Discord. Every message is untrusted.

s9.2
00:03:14.835 --> 00:03:19.038
Its agents can only read and search. They have no bash, edit or write.

s9.3
00:03:19.038 --> 00:03:24.113
They hold no chat, tracker or App credential, and read code with a read-only token.

s9.4
00:03:24.113 --> 00:03:27.572
An agent can propose tickets, but a proposal creates nothing.

s9.5
00:03:27.572 --> 00:03:31.394
Only a person mapped in notify.users can confirm.

s10.1
00:03:32.844 --> 00:03:36.955
Local mode has no container. The agent runs as the host user.

s10.2
00:03:36.955 --> 00:03:42.528
Symphony strips its own secrets from the agent's environment. That is hygiene, not a boundary.

s10.3
00:03:42.528 --> 00:03:46.766
With a GitHub App, each attempt still gets a scoped token in a private file.

s10.4
00:03:46.766 --> 00:03:50.853
There is no NetworkPolicy, and your own MCP config still loads.

s10.5
00:03:50.853 --> 00:03:55.589
Use a dedicated user, and run only tickets from people you trust with a shell.
